<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salamis, Cyprus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Constantia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salamis was an ancient city on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, north of modern Famagusta, founded in the 11th century BCE as successor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enkomi">Enkomi</a>. Its kings were often the strongest on the island, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evagoras-i">Evagoras I</a> in the 4th century BCE. As Constantia it was the metropolitan see of Cyprus in Late Antiquity, and it was abandoned after Arab raids in the 7th century; extensive ruins survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus.jpg" alt="Salamis, Cyprus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerhard Haubold · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gymnasion_und_Therme_aus_S%C3%BCdwest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
